{"entries":[{"caption":"Figure 6.1. Simulated API response time under increasing request load with and without a cache. Without caching, response time grows steeply as the database becomes the bottleneck: each request executes a full spatial query, and database contention increases nonlinearly near saturation. With caching (60% hit rate), most requests are served from Redis at low, flat latency; only cache misses reach the database. The database sees roughly 40% of the total request rate, delaying the onset of saturation substantially.","key":"fig-cache-response-time","order":{"number":1,"section":[7,5,4,0,0,0,0]}}],"headings":["the-puzzle","rest-vs-ogc-the-standards-layer","ogc-api-features","ogc-api-maps","why-conformance-matters","fastapi-for-spatial-services","pydantic-schemas-for-spatial-responses","dependency-injection-for-database-connections","access-patterns-and-query-plans","bounding-box-queries","point-in-polygon","nearest-neighbour","caching","materialised-views","api-layer-caching-with-redis","http-cache-headers","simulated-response-time-cached-vs-uncached","rate-limiting-and-pagination","cursor-based-pagination","rate-limiting-middleware","what-to-try","exercises","build-this"],"options":{"chapters":true}}